WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.116
[MUSIC]

00:00:08.116 --> 00:00:10.417
Most of us, when we think about
negotiation,

00:00:10.417 --> 00:00:13.760
have already started putting on the battle
armor.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:18.440
We see it as a battle, and we see the
battle being,

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.520
how can I get things from you that you
don't wanna give me, and

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:24.430
how can I keep you from getting things
that I don't want you to have?

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:30.992
There are, of course, negotiations like
that, but most of the social interactions

00:00:30.992 --> 00:00:35.521
that we engage in, where negotiation is an
opportunity, are not like that.

00:00:35.521 --> 00:00:39.177
But if we bring that perspective, that
filter,

00:00:39.177 --> 00:00:43.113
to the table we create an adversarial
interaction.

00:00:43.113 --> 00:00:46.039
[MUSIC]

00:00:46.039 --> 00:00:47.330
So in contrast,

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:53.430
we suggest that you move from this battle
mentality to one of fluency.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:56.860
And what I mean by fluency is, is that you
move to thinking about well,

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:59.770
what am I trying to achieve in this
negotiation?

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:04.010
And what are the strategies and tactics
that will help me meet that goal?

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:09.625
And what that means is, is that sometimes
while you might have a situation that

00:01:09.625 --> 00:01:15.582
is a negotiation, you're gonna be so much
better off if you engage not in a battle,

00:01:15.582 --> 00:01:19.605
but in a collaborative, problem-solving
endeavor.

00:01:19.605 --> 00:01:23.527
Now, not just any problem-solving, so it's
not just any solution, and

00:01:23.527 --> 00:01:26.720
I think that's really important to
understand.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:31.480
There are particular type of solutions
that we suggest you focus,

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:36.240
and that, that focus is, I want a solution
that makes me better off

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:41.013
than I would have been with my status quo,
or with my alternative.

00:01:41.013 --> 00:01:45.375
So, it's gotta be superior to my status
quo, superior to my alternatives, and

00:01:45.375 --> 00:01:48.266
because negotiation is an interdependent
process,

00:01:48.266 --> 00:01:50.920
there is no commanding control in
negotiation.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:53.362
I can't say, you will accept that.

00:01:53.362 --> 00:01:58.462
I've also got to find a solution that not
only makes me better off, but

00:01:58.462 --> 00:02:04.689
from my counterpart's perspective, allows
them to be whole or perhaps better off,

00:02:04.689 --> 00:02:10.265
because they have to willingly agree for a
negotiation agreement to occur.

00:02:10.265 --> 00:02:14.247
[MUSIC]

00:02:14.247 --> 00:02:15.770
People will tell you what is hard for
them.

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:18.210
They will tell you what is easy for them.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:20.010
But what most people do in negotiations,

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:23.250
you're so caught up in trying your own
perspective in figuring out,

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:27.820
how am I gonna force this issue, but you
don't listen to what they have to say.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:31.220
And because this is about influence, I
mean seriously,

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:34.110
when was the last time you could have any
command and control?

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:35.550
True command and control.

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:38.010
It doesn't matter where you are in the
hierarchy.

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:41.050
The CEO doesn't really have command and
control.

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:42.230
They can say do this.

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:45.230
Parents don't have command and control.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:49.500
They can say do this and the kids are
like, maybe, maybe not.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.090
So are employees.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:54.168
So, what I've gotta be able to do is
influence people in directions,

00:02:54.168 --> 00:02:56.934
which means I need to understand what
their interests are, and

00:02:56.934 --> 00:02:58.831
I need to understand what motivates them.

00:02:58.831 --> 00:03:02.654
And those are the kinds of questions that
I would ask in the negotiation, but

00:03:02.654 --> 00:03:06.678
it's also the kinds of questions I would
try to answer before the negotiation.

00:03:06.678 --> 00:03:09.953
[MUSIC]

00:03:09.953 --> 00:03:14.129
If you wanna put successful negotiators
and less successful negotiators into two

00:03:14.129 --> 00:03:18.182
camps, one of the thing that will probably
help you figure out which camp people

00:03:18.182 --> 00:03:20.620
belong in is the quality of their
preparation.

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:24.570
It is probably the single most

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:29.260
important factor that you can control in a
negotiation, how prepared are you?

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:34.200
Most negotiators are woefully
underprepared.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.960
Too many of us are confident that we can
just flow with it,

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:40.260
that we'll figure it out, because it's
very simple.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:42.940
I don't want you to have whatever it is
you want, and

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:44.730
I want whatever it is you don't want me to
have.

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:50.600
Negotiation is so much more complex and so

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:56.040
much more powerful than those simple
metrics of a pitched battle.

00:03:57.350 --> 00:03:59.930
Planning and preparation can help you
figure out,

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:03.718
what are some creative proposals that you
might make to your counterpart?

00:04:03.718 --> 00:04:08.272
Based upon the unique information that you
have and based on your understanding of

00:04:08.272 --> 00:04:11.902
your counterpart, that might open up new
solutions that alone,

00:04:11.902 --> 00:04:14.349
neither one of you could have implemented.

00:04:14.349 --> 00:04:14.849
[MUSIC]

